---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import births from "../data/mazza-births.json";
const found = births.filter((b) => b.found);
const nulls = births.filter((b) => !b.found);
---
<Base title="The Sicilian Registers" eyebrow="Read 10 September 2026"
      dek="Nine annual indexes across three comuni, hunting one man. He is not in any of them — and five other Mazza births are, two of them read in full.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">The family tree says <strong>Salvatore Mazza was born at Piedimonte Etneo in
    1879</strong>. He is the second generation of the surname line, and his act of birth would name
    his father <P n="Rosario Mazza" /> with an age and a trade — the one document that could join the
    <a href={u("/mazza")}>hundred and ten unattached Mazza</a> to this family.</p>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1rem">The volumes exist, they are digitised, and their
    alphabetical indexes have now been read. <strong>He is not in any of them.</strong></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What was read</h2>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m">Year</th><th>Comune</th><th class="m">Act</th><th>Mazza birth</th><th>Father</th><th>Where the act says they lived</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{births.map((b) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="m">{b.year}</td>
            <td>{b.comune}</td>
            <td class="m">{b.act}</td>
            <td>{b.found ? <strong>{b.name}</strong> : <span style="color:var(--ink-3)">no Mazza in the index</span>}</td>
            <td>{b.father !== "—" ? `${b.father}, ${b.fatherAge}` : "—"}</td>
            <td>{b.street !== "—" ? b.street : "—"}</td>
          </tr>))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="cap">Nine annual indexes: Piedimonte Etneo 1877–1881, Mascali 1879, Giarre 1879.
    Archivio di Stato di Catania, Stato civile italiano, via the Antenati portal. Every one of these
    volumes carries an alphabetical <em>Indice Annuale</em> in its last pages, which is how five years
    of one comune and two more comunes were checked without reading a single register through.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The finding</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">No Salvatore — but three Mazza men on one street</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.9rem 0 1.4rem">The two 1879 acts were read in full, and between them
    they document a Mazza kindred living on <strong>Via Terremorte</strong> in Piedimonte Etneo:</p>
    <div class="rec">
      <div class="k">Act 167</div>
      <div class="v"><strong>Mazza Pasquale</strong>, <strong>fifty</strong> — born about 1829 — a
      <em>bracciante</em>, with his wife <strong>Greco Concetta</strong>, <em>industriosa</em>.
      Daughter <strong>Rosaria</strong> born 28 October 1879 at half past six in the morning.</div>
      <div class="w">29 Oct 1879</div>
      <div class="k">Act 205</div>
      <div class="v"><strong>Mazza Salvatore</strong>, <strong>thirty-four</strong> — born about 1845 —
      a <em>bracciante</em>, with his wife <strong>Greco Concetta</strong>, <em>industriosa</em>.
      Son <strong>Venerio</strong> born 12 December 1879 at half past five in the morning.</div>
      <div class="w">14 Dec 1879</div>
      <div class="k">Witness</div>
      <div class="v"><strong>Mazza Antonino</strong>, <strong>thirty-five</strong> — born about 1844 —
      a <em>bracciante</em> of the same comune, who signs act 205.</div>
      <div class="w">14 Dec 1879</div>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:24px"><strong>And an oddity the archive will not smooth over.</strong>
    Two different men — Pasquale, fifty, and Salvatore, thirty-four — each declare a child born on
    <strong>Via Terremorte</strong> to a wife named <strong>Greco Concetta</strong>, six weeks apart.
    One woman cannot have borne both. So either they are two women of the same very common name
    married to two Mazza men on one street — brothers, or cousins, which is entirely ordinary in a
    Sicilian town — or one of the two acts has been misread. The acts are reproduced below so anyone
    can check.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Act 167 · Piedimonte Etneo, Nati 1879</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Rosaria Mazza, 28 October 1879</h2>
    <div class="hero-plate" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <img src={u("/plates/act167-mazza-rosaria.jpg")} alt="Piedimonte Etneo birth register 1879, act 167" />
      <div>
        <blockquote style="margin:0 0 .9rem">…è comparso <strong>Mazza Pasquale</strong>, di anni
        <strong>cinquanta</strong>, <strong>bracciante</strong>, domiciliato in Piedimonte Etneo… del
        dì <strong>ventotto</strong>… nella casa posta in <strong>Via Terremorte</strong> al numero
        senza, da <strong>Greco Concetta, industriosa, sua moglie, seco lui convivente</strong>… è
        nato un bambino di sesso femminino… a cui dà il nome di <strong>Rosaria</strong>.</blockquote>
        <p>A note added in the margin twenty-four years later records that she
        <strong>married Giuseppe Monforte, son of the late Salvatore, on 26 April 1903</strong>.</p>
        <p class="small" style="margin-top:.8rem">Archivio di Stato di Catania, Stato civile italiano,
        Piedimonte Etneo, Nati 1879, atto 167 — Antenati <code>an_ua83492</code>, image 74.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Act 205 · Piedimonte Etneo, Nati 1879, volume 2</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Venerio Mazza, 12 December 1879</h2>
    <div class="hero-plate" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <img src={u("/plates/act205-mazza-venerio.jpg")} alt="Piedimonte Etneo birth register 1879, act 205" />
      <div>
        <blockquote style="margin:0 0 .9rem">…è comparso <strong>Mazza Salvatore</strong>, di anni
        <strong>trentaquattro</strong>, <strong>bracciante</strong>, domiciliato in Piedimonte Etneo…
        del dì <strong>dodici</strong>… nella casa posta in <strong>Via Terremorte</strong> al numero
        senza, da <strong>Greco Concetta, industriosa, sua moglie</strong>… è nato un bambino di sesso
        mascolino… a cui dà il nome di <strong>Venerio</strong>.</blockquote>
        <p>Witnessed by <strong>Mazza Antonino</strong>, thirty-five, a <em>bracciante</em>, and
        <strong>Morabito Angelo</strong>, thirty-eight. A marginal note records Venerio's marriage to
        <strong>Rosaria Greco, daughter of Carmelo, on 16 November 1908</strong>.</p>
        <p class="small" style="margin-top:.8rem">Antenati <code>an_ua83493</code> — the 1879
        supplement, segnatura 8957. The main volume ran out of pages around act 175; the annual index
        covers both books, which is why act 205 is indexed in one and written in the other.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>What the null means</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Salvatore Mazza's birth — "1879, Piedimonte Etneo" — is <strong>disputed</strong>: asserted in
      the family record, and absent from <strong>five consecutive years</strong> of the register that
      should hold it, and from the two neighbouring comuni the family also appears in. What remains:</p>
      <ul style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14px;line-height:1.75;color:var(--ink-2);padding-left:20px">
        <li><strong>A wider year range at Piedimonte Etneo</strong> — 1875, 1876, and 1882 onward. The
        volumes are digitised and each carries its own index, so this is index reads, not volume reads.</li>
        <li><strong>Catania itself</strong>, where he died in 1929, and where a large town's registers
        are a much bigger job.</li>
        <li><strong>That the tree has a generation compressed.</strong> There <em>is</em> a Salvatore
        Mazza of Piedimonte Etneo — a day-labourer of Via Terremorte, born about 1845 — but he is a
        father in 1879, not a newborn. Whether he is an ancestor, and how, is exactly the kind of thing
        a marriage act would settle.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="margin-top:14px">Until one of those lands, the head of the Mazza line stays where it
      was: a man called Rosario about whom one fact is known.
      <a href={u("/open-questions")}>The queue →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
